================================================================================
Algorithms distilled into native Go (clean-room, NO source code used)
================================================================================

The following projects inspired ideas that were re-authored from scratch in Go.
No source code from them is included in or distributed with Prefex. These are
courtesy credits, not license obligations:

  - RouteLLM            (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/lm-sys/RouteLLM
  - LLMLingua / -2      (MIT)         https://github.com/microsoft/LLMLingua
  - caveman-compression (MIT)         https://github.com/wilpel/caveman-compression
  - RTK                 (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/rtk-ai/rtk
  - Mem0                (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/mem0ai/mem0
  - MemGPT / Letta      (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/letta-ai/letta
  - OpenHands           (MIT, core)   https://github.com/OpenHands/OpenHands
  - GraphRAG            (MIT)         https://github.com/microsoft/graphrag
  - vLLM                (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm
  - Ollama              (MIT)         https://github.com/ollama/ollama
  - headroom            (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/headroomlabs-ai/headroom
      (algorithms reimplemented in Go: content-routed compaction, recover-on-demand
       CCR, output shaping. The headroom Kompress *model* is run via the optional
       sidecar below, not reimplemented.)
  - codeburn            (MIT)         https://github.com/getagentseal/codeburn
      (ideas: dead-MCP-tool detection thresholds, cache-aware waste pricing, the
       embedded-pricing-snapshot approach. Re-authored as Setup Health.)
  - Invariant Scanner   (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/invariantlabs-ai/invariant
      (idea: typed violation taxonomy for agent tool-call traces. Re-authored as
       the live policy gateway's Rego policy bundle; theirs is post-hoc trace
       analysis, ours gates on the wire.)
  - DefenseClaw (Cisco) (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/cisco-ai-defense/defenseclaw
      (ideas: tool-result content scanning, MCP tool-description admission
       scanning, severity-tiered outcomes, observe-then-enforce workflow.
       Re-authored in Go + Rego on prefex's wire position; their fail-closed
       posture and YAML policy schema deliberately not adopted.)
  - Chain of Draft      (paper)       https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.18600
      (idea: draft-then-refine prompting to cut reasoning tokens. Re-authored as
       the effort/verbosity steer appended after the last cache_control
       breakpoint, so the cached prefix is never disturbed.)
  - Switchyard (NVIDIA) (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/NVIDIA-NeMo/Switchyard
      (idea: a TYPED classifier taxonomy for routing decisions — task_type,
       complexity, reasoning_depth, risk — rather than a single score. Re-authored
       as the advisor's typed schema. Their Rust wire-format translation layer was
       reviewed and not ported; prefex-auto reuses prefex's own translation.)
  - llmfit              (MIT)         https://github.com/AlexsJones/llmfit
      (idea: scoring candidate local models against detected hardware by weighted
       fit — VRAM, RAM headroom, disk, GPU speed class. Re-authored as
       internal/modelfit against internal/hardware's detected profile.)
  - OmniRoute           (MIT)         https://github.com/diegosouzapw/OmniRoute
      (ideas: an evidence-tagged, dated catalogue of free-tier plans; rate
       limits learned from response headers per key; a per-key circuit
       breaker. Re-authored as the prefex-auto free-pool catalogue and quota
       learner in Go. Its consumer-login providers, client fingerprinting,
       price table and advisory-only ToS labels were deliberately not adopted.)
  - Personal AI Router (NVIDIA) (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/NVIDIA/Personal-AI-Router
      (ideas: hysteresis bands on load thresholds; stale telemetry read as
       unknown, never zero; placing work on machines on your own network.
       Re-authored as prefex-auto's quota tracking and local-server capacity.)
  - Experiential        (Apache-2.0)  https://github.com/experientiallabs/experiential
      (ideas: paired-difference estimation with a variance floor — a constant
       sample cannot report zero uncertainty; and coerce-with-disclosure, where
       every request substitution is named as path->effective, logged and
       counted rather than applied silently. Both re-authored in Go. Their
       kNN-over-embeddings routing was reviewed and deliberately not adopted.)
  - Token Meter (Splunk)(MIT)         https://github.com/splunk/token-meter
      (ideas: evidence semantics, where every displayed number carries its own
       provenance — covered / partial / estimated / unavailable — and a period
       with no comparable prior renders "no comparable prior period" rather
       than a zero delta; a run-first information architecture that leads with
       the active session; and a single multi-mode execution chart in place of
       several single-purpose ones. Re-authored in Go and vanilla JS against
       our own on-wire data; their cost figures, runtime-adapter architecture
       and Git delivery page were reviewed and deliberately not adopted.)

================================================================================
Third-party components bundled or invoked by the OPTIONAL Kompress sidecar
================================================================================

These apply ONLY if you opt into the text-compaction sidecar
(compression.kompress: true). The sidecar runs out of process; the Prefex
binary itself stays model-free. See deploy/kompress-sidecar/.

  headroom / Kompress (model + ONNX inference run by the sidecar)
    headroom's algorithms are reimplemented in Go (credited above); this entry
    is the keep/drop model the sidecar runs.
    License: Apache-2.0
    https://github.com/headroomlabs-ai/headroom

  chopratejas/kompress-v2-base
    ModernBERT keep/drop model weights served by the sidecar (downloaded from
    Hugging Face at deploy time; not redistributed by Prefex).
    License: Apache-2.0
    https://huggingface.co/chopratejas/kompress-v2-base

  ModernBERT (answerdotai/ModernBERT-base)
    The base encoder the kompress weights are fine-tuned from.
    License: Apache-2.0
    https://github.com/AnswerDotAI/ModernBERT

  ONNX Runtime
    Inference runtime for the model (no PyTorch).
    License: MIT
    https://github.com/microsoft/onnxruntime

  Hugging Face Transformers
    Tokenization for the sidecar.
    License: Apache-2.0
    https://github.com/huggingface/transformers

================================================================================
Third-party Go libraries compiled into the prefex binary
================================================================================

These are linked into the released binary and distributed with it:

  github.com/open-policy-agent/opa  (Apache-2.0)    embedded policy engine (policy gateway)
  github.com/redis/go-redis/v9   (BSD-2-Clause)    optional Redis session store
  gopkg.in/yaml.v3               (MIT, Apache-2.0)  config parsing
  modernc.org/sqlite             (BSD-3-Clause)     embedded dashboard database

The complete set of third-party Go modules compiled into the binary (46 modules,
direct and transitive — the growth over the earlier 22 is OPA's dependency
tree) and their licenses is listed in THIRD_PARTY_LICENSES.md.

Vendored source (third-party code carried in the Prefex source tree and compiled
into the binary, rather than pulled as a module):

  github.com/gpustack/gguf-parser-go  (MIT)  third_party/gguf-parser-go/
    GGUF model-file parsing, used to read local model metadata. The upstream
    LICENSE is retained in that directory.
    https://github.com/gpustack/gguf-parser-go

Embedded data:

  LiteLLM model_prices_and_context_window.json  (MIT)
    https://github.com/BerriAI/litellm — the model-pricing snapshot embedded in
    the binary and refreshed per release / via `prefex pricing refresh`.

